## Orion interactive photograph

Authentic image: Akira Fujii / ESA/Hubble opo0205b, 1598 × 2000, locally stored, full framing preserved. The default HTML and every fresh page load show no overlay. No saved toggle preference changes that default.

Seven identified star centers are recorded in `assets/orion-coordinates.json`. Their brightness-weighted coordinates were measured in the actual photograph and visually checked against the ESO/IAU/Sky & Telescope Orion chart. No invented positions or unrelated stars were used. The annotation follows the torso/Belt/leg pattern only; Orion's other stars are deliberately not traced. The lines are described as educational, imaginary connections.

Browser checks in the Codex in-app browser on this Mac:

| Viewport | Outcome |
| --- | --- |
| Desktop 1440 × 1000 | Photograph, rings, connecting lines and labels visually aligned. |
| Tablet portrait 768 × 1024 | Alignment retained; photo and overlay both 302.398 × 378.469 CSS px at the same origin. |
| Tablet landscape 1024 × 768 | Alignment retained; photo and overlay both 417.523 × 522.555 CSS px at the same origin. Toggle off/on leaves photo dimensions and origin unchanged. |
| Phone 390 × 844 | Single-column layout; photo and overlay both 331 × 414.266 CSS px at the same origin. Toggle touch target about 46.8px high. |

No horizontal document overflow in these checks. A shared intrinsic image ratio and SVG viewBox maintain the mapping. Screenshot evidence is retained in the workspace `qa/` folder.

Mouse click, Space and Enter toggle the native button. Visible label switches Show/Hide constellation lines; `aria-pressed` changes false/true; off hides only the SVG layer. Description changes with the state. Rings, lines, star labels and text descriptions avoid relying only on color. The SVG is decorative to assistive technology; the photograph has meaningful alternative text and the state description is live.

